.SH "NAME"
octa \- multi\-format tabular data viewer, editor, CLI tool, and MCP server
.SH "SYNOPSIS"
.sp
\fBocta\fP [\fIFILE\fP.\|.\|.]
.sp
\fBocta\fP \fIACTION\fP [\fIOPTIONS\fP]
.sp
\fBocta\fP \fB\-\-mcp\fP [\fB\-\-mcp\-read\-only\fP] [\fB\-\-mcp\-tools\fP \fILIST\fP] [\fB\-\-mcp\-without\fP \fILIST\fP]
.SH "DESCRIPTION"
.sp
\fBocta\fP is a desktop application for viewing and editing tabular data
files. It opens Parquet, CSV, JSON, SQLite, DuckDB, Excel and many more,
over 30 formats in all, in a spreadsheet\-like view with sorting,
filtering, full\-text search, inline editing, SQL queries, and file
comparison.
.sp
Run without an action flag, it launches the graphical interface and opens
each \fIFILE\fP in a tab. Run with one action flag, it performs that action,
prints the result and exits. The actions are listed below, grouped by
task. Only one action runs per invocation. Each action takes the files
shown in its usage line; any other trailing \fIFILE\fP is ignored with a
warning.
.sp
A \fIFILE\fP may be a local path, \fB\-\fP for standard input, a plain \fBhttp://\fP or
\fBhttps://\fP URL, or a cloud object URL (\f(CRs3://bucket/key\fP,
\f(CRaz://container/blob\fP, \f(CRgs://bucket/key\fP, \f(CRsftp://user@host/path\fP,
\f(CRdavs://host/path\fP). A URL or cloud object is downloaded to a temporary
file and read as usual. Cloud credentials come from a saved cloud
connection covering the URL, otherwise from the ambient chain (\f(CRAWS_*\fP
environment variables, a cached SSO session, \f(CRaz login\fP, Google
application default credentials). Cloud objects are read\-only here:
output still goes to a local path. See \fBPipelines\fP and \fBReading from a
URL\fP under EXAMPLES.
.sp
\fBocta \-\-help\fP prints the flag list, and \fBocta \-\-help\-all\fP adds worked
examples for every action.
.SH "ACTIONS"
.sp
Each entry shows the action\(cqs usage line, what it does, and the options
that belong to it alone. Options several actions share (\fB\-f\fP, \fB\-\-rows\fP,
\fB\-\-out\-dir\fP, \fB\-\-table\fP and others) are described under COMMON OPTIONS.
.SS "Look at a file"
.sp
\fB\-\-schema\fP \fIFILE\fP
.RS 4
Print the column schema of \fIFILE\fP as a two\-column table (column
name, data type). For streaming formats (Parquet, CSV, TSV) the reader
loads the initial\-row batch (\fB2,000,000 rows\fP by default) and projects
the schema from that.
.RE
.sp
\fB\-\-head\fP \fIFILE\fP [\fB\-n\fP \fIN\fP] [\fB\-\-sort\-by\fP \fICOLS\fP [\fB\-\-sort\-desc\fP \fICOLS\fP] [\fB\-\-natural\fP]]
.RS 4
Print the first \fIN\fP rows of \fIFILE\fP to standard output. \fIN\fP defaults
to 20 and is set with \fB\-n\fP / \fB\-\-lines\fP. For streaming formats the
reader stops at the initial\-load cap and \fIN\fP is a slice off that, so
this is fast on large files. With \fB\-\-sort\-by\fP the rows are sorted
before the first \fIN\fP are taken.
.RE
.sp
\fB\-\-tail\fP \fIFILE\fP [\fB\-n\fP \fIN\fP]
.RS 4
Print the last \fIN\fP rows of \fIFILE\fP. \fIN\fP defaults to 20 and is set with
\fB\-n\fP / \fB\-\-lines\fP. Streaming formats load with the standard row cap, so
the tail reflects the end of the loaded window; raise \fB\-\-rows\fP to tail
the true end of a very large file.
.RE
.sp
\fB\-\-sample\fP \fIFILE\fP [\fB\-n\fP \fIN\fP] [\fB\-\-seed\fP \fIN\fP]
.RS 4
Print a random \fIN\fP\-row sample of \fIFILE\fP (without replacement,
original row order preserved). \fIN\fP defaults to 20 (\fB\-n\fP / \fB\-\-lines\fP).
The sample is reproducible for a given \fB\-\-seed\fP (default \fB0\fP).
.RE
.sp
\fB\-\-describe\fP \fIFILE\fP [\fB\-\-table\fP \fINAME\fP] [\fB\-\-sample\-rows\fP \fIN\fP] [\fB\-\-deep\fP]
.RS 4
Print a one\-shot orientation snapshot of \fIFILE\fP: format, file
size, row count, column schema, and a small sample of rows. The
\fB\-\-table\fP flag picks a specific table on multi\-table sources.
.sp
\fB\-\-sample\-rows\fP \fIN\fP
.RS 4
Number of preview rows (default \fB5\fP, clamped to \fB100\fP).
.RE
.sp
\fB\-\-deep\fP
.RS 4
Also report how the file is physically written: row groups,
compression codec, encodings and per\-column statistics, followed by
one row per column per row group. Plain\-language hints about a poor
layout (very small row groups, missing statistics, no compression)
go to standard error, so a piped run stays parseable. Parquet
reports full detail; other formats report their size and state that
they expose no inspectable structure. Place it after the file:
\fB\-\-describe FILE \-\-deep\fP.
.RE
.RE
.SS "Analyse"
.sp
\fB\-\-report\fP \fIOUT.html\fP \fIFILE\fP [\fB\-\-report\-sample\fP \fIN\fP] [\fB\-\-report\-sections\fP \fILIST\fP]
.RS 4
Write an HTML profiling report for \fIFILE\fP to \fIOUT.html\fP: per\-column
statistics, distribution charts, the most common values and a
correlation matrix. The document is self\-contained (inline CSS,
inline SVG, no JavaScript) and fetches nothing, so it can be mailed
or published as\-is.
.sp
\fB\-\-report\-sample\fP \fIN\fP
.RS 4
Profile a random sample of \fIN\fP rows instead of every row. The report
states that it sampled.
.RE
.sp
\fB\-\-report\-sections\fP \fILIST\fP
.RS 4
A comma\-separated subset of \fBstats\fP, \fBdistributions\fP, \fBtop_values\fP
and \fBcorrelation\fP (default: all four). An unknown name is an error
rather than a silent omission.
.RE
.RE
.sp
\fB\-\-unique\-columns\fP \fIFILE\fP [\fB\-\-table\fP \fINAME\fP] [\fB\-\-max\-combo\fP \fIN\fP]
.RS 4
Find columns (and optional small combinations) whose values are
unique across \fIFILE\fP. Useful for primary\-key reconnaissance on
undocumented data.
.sp
\fB\-\-max\-combo\fP \fIN\fP
.RS 4
Also test column pairs (\fB2\fP) or triples (\fB3\fP). Default \fB1\fP, clamped
to \fB[1, 3]\fP.
.RE
.RE
.sp
\fB\-\-shapes\fP \fIFILE\fP \fB\-\-shapes\-column\fP \fICOL\fP [\fB\-\-shapes\-exact\fP]
.RS 4
What a column\(cqs values look like, digits folded to \fB9\fP, capitals to
\fBA\fP, other letters to \fBa\fP, punctuation kept, and a run of letters of
one kind written once (\fBAnna Schmidt\fP is \fBAa Aa\fP). One row per shape,
most common first, with a count and an example.
.sp
\fB\-\-shapes\-column\fP \fICOL\fP
.RS 4
Column to shape. Required.
.RE
.sp
\fB\-\-shapes\-exact\fP
.RS 4
Shape letter by letter (\fBAaaa Aaaaaaa\fP) instead of by word.
.RE
.RE
.sp
\fB\-\-lookups\fP \fIFILE\fP [\fB\-\-lookups\-min\fP \fIN\fP]
.RS 4
Hidden lookup tables: columns that always follow another column. One
row per key and following column, with its consistency, conflicting
keys and breaking rows.
.sp
\fB\-\-lookups\-min\fP \fIN\fP
.RS 4
Share of rows (0 to 1) that must agree with their key. Default 0.95.
.RE
.RE
.sp
\fB\-\-outliers\fP \fIFILE\fP [\fB\-\-outlier\-method\fP \fIM\fP] [\fB\-\-outlier\-cols\fP \fICOLS\fP] [\fB\-\-outlier\-k\fP \fIK\fP]
.RS 4
Flag numeric outlier cells in \fIFILE\fP and print their coordinates.
.sp
\fB\-\-outlier\-method\fP \fIM\fP
.RS 4
\fBiqr\fP (default) or \fBzscore\fP.
.RE
.sp
\fB\-\-outlier\-cols\fP \fICOLS\fP
.RS 4
Comma\-separated columns to scan (default: all).
.RE
.sp
\fB\-\-outlier\-k\fP \fIK\fP
.RS 4
Threshold multiplier (default \fB1.5\fP for IQR, \fB3.0\fP for z\-score).
.RE
.RE
.sp
\fB\-\-detect\-pii\fP \fIFILE\fP [\fB\-\-pii\-sample\fP \fIN\fP]
.RS 4
Scan \fIFILE\fP for likely PII columns (email, phone, IBAN, credit card,
SSN) and print the findings.
.sp
\fB\-\-pii\-sample\fP \fIN\fP
.RS 4
Rows sampled per column (default \fB500\fP).
.RE
.RE
.sp
\fB\-\-compare\-distributions\fP \fIFILE\fP \fB\-\-dist\-column\fP \fICOL\fP [\fB\-\-dist\-file\-b\fP \fIFILE\fP] [\fB\-\-dist\-column\-b\fP \fICOL\fP]
.RS 4
Do two columns look like the same population? Numbers are compared
with a two\-sample Kolmogorov\-Smirnov test, which looks at the whole
shape rather than one summary of it; anything else is compared as
categories with a chi\-square test. Prints a two\-column \fBfield\fP /
\fBvalue\fP table led by a plain\-language \fBheadline\fP (the answer), then
the \fBverdict\fP (\fBsame\fP or \fBdifferent\fP at p = 0.05), the test, its
statistic and p\-value. When the test does not apply (fewer than 20
usable values, or more than 50 distinct ones), a \fBresult\fP row says
why instead of a verdict. Rare categories are pooled into one
\fB(other)\fP bucket rather than dropped, and nulls are left out.
\fB\-\-table\-a\fP / \fB\-\-table\-b\fP pick the table on multi\-table sources.
.sp
\fB\-\-dist\-column\fP \fICOL\fP
.RS 4
The column to compare. Required.
.RE
.sp
\fB\-\-dist\-file\-b\fP \fIFILE\fP
.RS 4
A second file. Omit it to compare two columns of the first.
.RE
.sp
\fB\-\-dist\-column\-b\fP \fICOL\fP
.RS 4
The second column. Defaults to \fB\-\-dist\-column\fP.
.RE
.RE
.sp
\fB\-\-forecast\fP \fIFILE\fP \fB\-\-forecast\-x\fP \fICOL\fP \fB\-\-forecast\-y\fP \fICOL\fP [\fB\-\-forecast\-periods\fP \fIN\fP] [\fB\-\-forecast\-season\fP \fIN\fP]
.RS 4
Forecast one column over time (Holt\-Winters) with 80% and 95%
ranges.
.sp
\fB\-\-forecast\-x\fP \fICOL\fP
.RS 4
Time column: dates, date\-times or numbers, evenly spaced. Required.
.RE
.sp
\fB\-\-forecast\-y\fP \fICOL\fP
.RS 4
Column of numbers to forecast. Required.
.RE
.sp
\fB\-\-forecast\-periods\fP \fIN\fP
.RS 4
How many periods ahead. Default 12.
.RE
.sp
\fB\-\-forecast\-season\fP \fIN\fP
.RS 4
Season length in points. Default: read from the spacing.
.RE
.RE
.sp
\fB\-\-resample\fP \fICOL\fP \fIFILE\fP \fB\-\-value\-cols\fP \fICOLS\fP [\fB\-\-interval\fP \fII\fP] [\fB\-\-agg\fP \fIA\fP] [\fB\-\-group\-by\fP \fICOLS\fP]
.RS 4
Group \fIFILE\fP into time buckets: one row per \fB\-\-interval\fP of \fICOL\fP.
The time column is cast with \f(CRTRY_CAST\fP, so a row that will not parse
buckets as NULL instead of failing the whole run.
.sp
\fB\-\-value\-cols\fP \fICOLS\fP
.RS 4
The columns to aggregate. Required.
.RE
.sp
\fB\-\-interval\fP \fII\fP
.RS 4
\fBminute\fP, \fBhour\fP, \fBday\fP (default), \fBweek\fP, \fBmonth\fP, \fBquarter\fP or
\fByear\fP.
.RE
.sp
\fB\-\-agg\fP \fIA\fP
.RS 4
\fBsum\fP (default), \fBmean\fP, \fBmin\fP, \fBmax\fP, \fBcount\fP, \fBfirst\fP or \fBlast\fP.
.RE
.sp
\fB\-\-group\-by\fP \fICOLS\fP
.RS 4
Extra grouping columns, giving one series per combination.
.RE
.RE
.sp
\fB\-\-rolling\fP \fICOL\fP \fIFILE\fP \fB\-\-window\fP \fIN\fP \fB\-\-order\-by\fP \fICOL\fP [\fB\-\-agg\fP \fIA\fP] [\fB\-\-partition\-by\-cols\fP \fICOLS\fP]
.RS 4
Add a rolling aggregate of \fICOL\fP to \fIFILE\fP.
.sp
\fB\-\-window\fP \fIN\fP
.RS 4
Rows in the frame, including the current row.
.RE
.sp
\fB\-\-order\-by\fP \fICOL\fP
.RS 4
Column that orders the frame. Required: a rolling aggregate over
unordered rows is meaningless.
.RE
.sp
\fB\-\-agg\fP \fIA\fP
.RS 4
\fBmean\fP (default), \fBsum\fP, \fBmin\fP, \fBmax\fP, \fBcount\fP, \fBfirst\fP or \fBlast\fP.
.RE
.sp
\fB\-\-partition\-by\-cols\fP \fICOLS\fP
.RS 4
Restart the frame per group. Spelled that way because
\fB\-\-partition\-by\fP is the split\-into\-files action.
.RE
.RE
.sp
\fB\-\-overlaps\fP \fIFILE\fP [\fB\-\-overlaps\-start\fP \fICOL\fP] [\fB\-\-overlaps\-end\fP \fICOL\fP] [\fB\-\-overlaps\-lane\fP \fICOL\fP] [\fB\-\-overlaps\-label\fP \fICOL\fP]
.RS 4
Rows whose time spans overlap inside a lane (two bookings of one
room). One row per pair on stdout; \fBexits 1 when any is found\fP.
Spans that only touch do not overlap. Without \fB\-\-overlaps\-start\fP /
\fB\-\-overlaps\-end\fP the first two date columns are used.
.sp
\fB\-\-overlaps\-start\fP \fICOL\fP, \fB\-\-overlaps\-end\fP \fICOL\fP
.RS 4
Start and end columns. No end makes every row a point.
.RE
.sp
\fB\-\-overlaps\-lane\fP \fICOL\fP
.RS 4
Only rows with the same value in \fICOL\fP can overlap.
.RE
.sp
\fB\-\-overlaps\-label\fP \fICOL\fP
.RS 4
A column shown beside each row of a pair.
.RE
.RE
.sp
\fB\-\-cell\-history\fP \fIFILE\fP \fB\-\-history\-column\fP \fICOL\fP (\fB\-\-history\-key\fP \fICOLS\fP \fB\-\-history\-value\fP \fIVALUES\fP | \fB\-\-history\-row\fP \fIN\fP) [\fB\-\-history\-depth\fP \fIN\fP]
.RS 4
The commits that changed one cell of a file in a Git repository,
newest first, following the row by key and the file through renames.
.sp
\fB\-\-history\-column\fP \fICOL\fP
.RS 4
Column of the cell. Required.
.RE
.sp
\fB\-\-history\-key\fP \fICOLS\fP
.RS 4
Comma\-separated key column(s) that identify the row.
.RE
.sp
\fB\-\-history\-value\fP \fIVALUES\fP
.RS 4
Comma\-separated values of the row in each key column.
.RE
.sp
\fB\-\-history\-row\fP \fIN\fP
.RS 4
1\-based row number, when there is no key.
.RE
.sp
\fB\-\-history\-depth\fP \fIN\fP
.RS 4
How many commits to read, newest first. Default 50.
.RE
.RE
.sp
\fB\-\-relationships\fP \fIDIR\fP [\fB\-\-recursive\fP]
.RS 4
Rank the likely relationships between the tables in \fIDIR\fP and print
them best first: which column of which table lines up with which
column of which other table. Names take no part in the ranking; the
score comes from how much the values overlap, weighted by how
distinct each side is, so a status column cannot outrank a real key.
Each row also carries \fBorphans\fP, the number of distinct values on
the left with no partner on the right, which is what separates two
candidates that overlap identically. Reads values, so it is capped
at 30 files and at 10,000 rows per table. Unreadable files are noted
on stderr. Always exits \fB0\fP: this is a report, not a gate. Add
\fB\-\-recursive\fP to walk subdirectories.
.RE
.SS "Compare and check"
.sp
\fB\-\-diff\fP \fIFILE_A\fP \fIFILE_B\fP [\fB\-\-diff\-mode\fP \fIMODE\fP] [\fB\-\-diff\-on\fP \fICOLS\fP]
.RS 4
Compare two files, or with \fB\-\-diff\-db\fP a file and a live database
table. \fB\-\-diff\-mode\fP selects the strategy; the output is a table
tagged by a leading \fBstatus\fP column, and a summary line (per\-mode
counts) is written to standard error. Complements \fB\-\-compare\-schemas\fP,
which diffs only the column metadata.
.sp
\fB\-\-diff\-mode\fP \fIMODE\fP
.RS 4
Comparison strategy (default \fBset\fP):
\fBset\fP compares rows by whole\-row content (every column, positionally)
and prints rows unique to each side (\fBonly_in_a\fP / \fBonly_in_b\fP); the
files should share the same column order.
\fBordered\fP lines up row \fIi\fP of A with row \fIi\fP of B, prints matched rows
that differ as \fBchanged\fP (with a \fBchanged_columns\fP column) plus any
trailing \fBonly_in_a\fP / \fBonly_in_b\fP rows.
\fBjoin\fP matches rows on the \fB\-\-diff\-on\fP key column(s) and prints
added (\fBonly_in_b\fP), removed (\fBonly_in_a\fP), and \fBchanged\fP rows.
.RE
.sp
\fB\-\-diff\-on\fP \fICOLS\fP
.RS 4
Key column(s) for \fB\-\-diff\-mode join\fP, comma\-separated (matched by
name). Required when the mode is \fBjoin\fP; ignored otherwise.
.RE
.sp
\fB\-\-diff\-tolerance\fP \fIN\fP
.RS 4
Numbers within this absolute difference count as the same. Ordered
and join modes only; set mode refuses it.
.RE
.sp
\fB\-\-diff\-tolerance\-pct\fP \fIP\fP
.RS 4
Numbers within this percentage of the larger value count as the
same. A pair passes if it is within either number tolerance.
.RE
.sp
\fB\-\-diff\-time\-tolerance\fP \fISECS\fP
.RS 4
Dates and times at most this many seconds apart count as the same. A
date counts as midnight.
.RE
.sp
\fB\-\-diff\-ignore\-case\fP
.RS 4
Compare text without regard to upper and lower case, join keys
included.
.RE
.sp
\fB\-\-diff\-ignore\-spaces\fP
.RS 4
Compare text trimmed, with runs of spaces as one, join keys included.
.RE
.sp
\fB\-\-diff\-ignore\-columns\fP \fICOLS\fP
.RS 4
Columns to leave out of the comparison, comma\-separated or repeated.
They still appear in the output.
.RE
.sp
\fB\-\-diff\-db\fP \fICONN\fP
.RS 4
Compare against a live database table instead of a second file.
\fICONN\fP names a saved database connection. With this set, \fB\-\-diff\fP
takes a single file: that file is side A and the table is side B,
so \fBonly_in_a\fP means "in the file, not the table". Requires
\fB\-\-diff\-db\-table\fP. The table is read under the usual row cap.
.RE
.sp
\fB\-\-diff\-db\-table\fP \fITABLE\fP
.RS 4
Table to compare against on the \fB\-\-diff\-db\fP connection, as
\fISCHEMA.TABLE\fP or \fICATALOG.SCHEMA.TABLE\fP (the three\-part form is for
Trino, Snowflake, Databricks and BigQuery). An unqualified name uses
the connection\(cqs own database.
.RE
.RE
.sp
\fB\-\-compare\-schemas\fP \fIFILE_A\fP \fIFILE_B\fP [\fB\-\-table\-a\fP \fINAME\fP] [\fB\-\-table\-b\fP \fINAME\fP]
.RS 4
Diff the column schemas of two files. Prints a four\-column table
(\fBstatus\fP / \fBcolumn\fP / \fBtype_a\fP / \fBtype_b\fP). \fBstatus\fP is one of
\fBcommon\fP, \fBonly_in_a\fP, \fBonly_in_b\fP, \fBtype_mismatch\fP. Matching is by
exact, case\-sensitive column name. For multi\-table sources, the
table to read from each side is selected via \fB\-\-table\-a\fP /
\fB\-\-table\-b\fP.
.RE
.sp
\fB\-\-compare\-folders\fP \fIFOLDER\fP \fIFOLDER\fP.\|.\|. [\fB\-\-no\-recursive\fP]
.RS 4
Compare two or more folders file by file. Each \fIFOLDER\fP is a local
path or a cloud URL (\fBs3://\fP, \fBaz://\fP, \fBgs://\fP, \fBsftp://\fP,
\fBdavs://\fP). Files are matched by their path inside each folder;
copies of different sizes are different without being read, and
copies of equal size are compared by a BLAKE3 hash of their
contents. Prints one row per file with a \fBstatus\fP of \fBsame\fP,
\fBdifferent\fP or \fBmissing\fP, the folders that have it and lack it, and
the size, modified time and hash per folder. Each folder is listed
up to the file limit from the settings (\fBfolder_compare_max_files\fP,
default 100,000); a folder cut short is named on stderr. Exit code
is \fB0\fP when every file is the same everywhere and \fB1\fP when anything
differs, is missing, or a folder hit the limit.
.sp
\fB\-\-no\-recursive\fP
.RS 4
Compare only the top level of each folder.
.RE
.RE
.sp
\fB\-\-drift\-report\fP \fIFILE_A\fP \fIFILE_B\fP [\fB\-\-fail\-on\fP \fISPEC\fP]
.RS 4
Compare two versions of the same dataset and report how it moved.
Columns are matched by name: one present on a single side is
reported as added or removed, and every shared column gets its null
rate, distinct count and, when numeric, its minimum, maximum and
mean compared. Columns with few enough distinct values also list the
category values that appeared and vanished. Every figure comes from
the same Summary pass the \fBAnalyse \(-> Summary\fP tab uses, so the two
can never disagree. The report goes to stdout; the row counts,
added and removed column names and the pass/fail summary go to
stderr, so a pipe stays parseable. Exit code is \fB0\fP unless
\fB\-\-fail\-on\fP was given and a gate was breached.
.sp
\fB\-\-fail\-on\fP \fISPEC\fP
.RS 4
Turn the report into a CI gate. \fISPEC\fP is a comma\-separated list of
\fImetric\fP:_change_ pairs, for example \f(CRnull_rate:0.05,rows:0.1\fP,
where \fIchange\fP is the largest relative move that still passes. A
metric that never appears in the report is simply never applied. A
baseline of zero that moved at all counts as an unbounded change,
so a null rate going from \fB0\fP to \fB0.5\fP breaches any gate. Metric
names are \fBrows\fP, \fBnull_rate\fP, \fBdistinct_count\fP, \fBmin\fP, \fBmax\fP and
\fBmean\fP.
.RE
.RE
.sp
\fB\-\-schema\-drift\fP \fIDIR\fP [\fB\-\-recursive\fP] [\fB\-\-ignore\-case\fP]
.RS 4
Scan \fIDIR\fP and report which files disagree about their columns.
Files are grouped by schema, largest group first, so the answer to
a folder of 500 parts is "497 look like this, 3 look like that".
The report table goes to stdout; the per\-variant file lists, any
unreadable files and the drifting column names go to stderr, so a
pipe stays parseable. Exit code is \fB0\fP when every file agrees and
\fB1\fP otherwise, which is CI\-pipeable. Add \fB\-\-recursive\fP to walk
subdirectories (depth 8) and \fB\-\-ignore\-case\fP to treat column names
differing only in case as the same column.
.RE
.sp
\fB\-\-validate\-schema\fP \fIFILE\fP \fB\-\-expect\-schema\fP \fISCHEMA_FILE\fP [\fB\-\-table\fP \fINAME\fP]
.RS 4
Check \fIFILE\fP\*(Aqs column schema against a JSON Schema. Exit code is \fB0\fP
on a clean match, \fB1\fP otherwise, which is CI\-pipeable. Schemas
produced by \fB\-\-export\-schema \-t json\-schema\fP round\-trip through this
command.
.sp
\fB\-\-expect\-schema\fP \fISCHEMA_FILE\fP
.RS 4
Path to the expected JSON Schema. Required.
.RE
.RE
.sp
\fB\-\-check\fP \fIFILE\fP \fB\-\-rules\fP \fIRULES.toml\fP
.RS 4
Check \fIFILE\fP\*(Aqs values against a rules file and report which rules
failed. The report goes to stdout with one row per failing rule, its
failure count and up to three offending values; the summary line
goes to stderr. Exit code is \fB1\fP on any violation \fBand\fP on any rule
that could not run, because a rules file whose columns have since
been renamed would otherwise report a clean run over checks it
silently skipped. The same rules file is written and read by the
\fBData \(-> Data validation.\|.\|.\fP dialog.
.sp
\fB\-\-rules\fP \fIFILE\fP
.RS 4
The rules file. Required. It is TOML, one \f(CR[[rule]]\fP table per
check, naming its column and one of the kinds \fBnot_null\fP, \fBunique\fP,
\fBrange\fP (with \fBmin\fP and \fBmax\fP), \fBregex\fP (with \fBpattern\fP) or
\fBmax_length\fP (with \fBmax_length\fP). A rule without a \f(CRcolumn\fP applies
to every column. Example:
.sp
.if n .RS 4
.nf
.fam C
[[rule]]
column = "order_id"
kind = "unique"

[[rule]]
column = "amount"
kind = "range"
min = 0
.fam
.fi
.if n .RE
.RE
.RE
.sp
\fB\-\-check\-references\fP \fIPARENT\fP \fB\-\-parent\-column\fP \fICOL\fP [\fB\-\-child\-file\fP \fIFILE\fP] \fB\-\-child\-column\fP \fICOL\fP
.RS 4
List the child rows whose foreign key has no matching parent, one row
per offending value (capped at 500 values; the counts in the summary
on stderr stay exact). \fBExits 1 when there are any\fP, so it works as a
CI gate. A null or empty key is not an orphan: in every relational
database it means "no parent", so those rows are counted separately
and do not fail the gate. Keys are compared as trimmed text, so \f(CR1\fP
from a CSV matches \f(CR1\fP from a database. \fB\-\-table\-a\fP / \fB\-\-table\-b\fP
pick the table on multi\-table sources.
.sp
\fB\-\-parent\-column\fP \fICOL\fP
.RS 4
The parent\(cqs key column. Required.
.RE
.sp
\fB\-\-child\-file\fP \fIFILE\fP
.RS 4
The child file. Omit it for a self\-reference, such as a
\f(CRmanager_id\fP pointing at \f(CRid\fP in the same table.
.RE
.sp
\fB\-\-child\-column\fP \fICOL\fP
.RS 4
The child\(cqs foreign\-key column. Required.
.RE
.RE
.SS "Clean and transform"
.sp
\fB\-\-dedupe\fP \fIFILE\fP [\fB\-\-dedupe\-on\fP \fICOLS\fP] [\fB\-\-dedupe\-keep\fP \fIWHICH\fP]
.RS 4
Remove duplicate rows from \fIFILE\fP and print the result to stdout.
.sp
\fB\-\-dedupe\-on\fP \fICOLS\fP
.RS 4
Comma\-separated key columns. Without it the whole row is the key.
.RE
.sp
\fB\-\-dedupe\-keep\fP \fIWHICH\fP
.RS 4
Which occurrence survives: \fBfirst\fP (default) or \fBlast\fP.
.RE
.RE
.sp
\fB\-\-impute\fP \fICOL\fP=\fISTRATEGY\fP \fIFILE\fP
.RS 4
Fill missing or empty cells of a column of \fIFILE\fP. Repeatable.
Strategies: \fBmean\fP, \fBmedian\fP, \fBmode\fP, \fBffill\fP, \fBbfill\fP,
\fBconst:VALUE\fP.
.RE
.sp
\fB\-\-anonymize\fP \fISPEC\fP \fIFILE\fP
.RS 4
Mask or scramble sensitive columns of \fIFILE\fP per the JSON \fISPEC\fP file
and print the sanitised table to stdout. The input file is never
modified. The spec lists per\-column rules (\fBhash\fP / \fBpartial_mask\fP /
\fBredact\fP / \fBfake\fP) plus an optional shared \fBsalt\fP; columns are named.
The same value plus the same salt always maps the same way, so
duplicates stay linked and a re\-run re\-joins to an earlier export.
.RE
.sp
\fB\-\-hash\-columns\fP \fICOL,COL,.\|.\|.\fP \fIFILE\fP [\fB\-\-hash\-algo\fP \fIALGO\fP] [\fB\-\-hash\-delimiter\fP \fITEXT\fP] [\fB\-\-hash\-null\fP \fITEXT\fP] [\fB\-\-hash\-trim\fP] [\fB\-\-hash\-upper\fP] [\fB\-\-hash\-name\fP \fINAME\fP]
.RS 4
Append a column holding a hash of the named columns and print the
table to stdout. The input file is never modified. The columns are
joined in the order given, with the delimiter between them, and every
value is turned into text first, whatever its type. The hash is
lowercase hex.
.sp
\fB\-\-hash\-algo\fP \fIALGO\fP
.RS 4
\fBmd5\fP (default), \fBsha256\fP or \fBsha512\fP.
.RE
.sp
\fB\-\-hash\-delimiter\fP \fITEXT\fP
.RS 4
Put between the values (default \fB|\fP). May be empty:
\fB\-\-hash\-delimiter ""\fP.
.RE
.sp
\fB\-\-hash\-null\fP \fITEXT\fP
.RS 4
Stands in for a NULL cell (default empty).
.RE
.sp
\fB\-\-hash\-trim\fP
.RS 4
Strip whitespace from each value before hashing it.
.RE
.sp
\fB\-\-hash\-upper\fP
.RS 4
Upper\-case each value before hashing it.
.RE
.sp
\fB\-\-hash\-name\fP \fINAME\fP
.RS 4
Name of the new column (default \f(CRhash_<columns>\fP). It must not exist
yet.
.RE
.RE
.sp
\fB\-\-recipe\fP \fIRECIPE\fP \fIFILE\fP [\fB\-\-recipe\-out\fP \fIFILE\fP]
.RS 4
Replay a recipe (a \fB.ocp\fP file, short for Octa reCiPe, recorded in
the GUI) on \fIFILE\fP. Every step runs by column name. When any step
cannot run, nothing is written, the skipped steps go to stderr and
the exit code is \fB1\fP.
.sp
\fB\-\-recipe\-out\fP \fIFILE\fP
.RS 4
Write the result to \fIFILE\fP instead of stdout.
.RE
.RE
.sp
\fB\-\-test\-data\fP \fIFILE\fP.\|.\|. [\fB\-\-test\-data\-rows\fP \fIN\fP] [\fB\-\-seed\fP \fIN\fP] [\fB\-\-test\-data\-out\fP \fIPATH\fP] [\fB\-\-test\-data\-rename\-categories\fP]
.RS 4
Generate new rows shaped like each \fIFILE\fP: the same columns, numbers
and dates from the real spread, fake names, emails, IBANs and IDs,
empty cells at the real rate. Several files are generated together so
links between them still join. The plan goes to stderr, with a note
for every small category column that keeps its real values.
.sp
\fB\-\-test\-data\-rows\fP \fIN\fP
.RS 4
Rows per table (default: as many as the real one).
.RE
.sp
\fB\-\-test\-data\-out\fP \fIPATH\fP
.RS 4
Output file for one input, or an existing folder for several
(written as \fIname_test.ext\fP). Without it, one table goes to stdout.
.RE
.sp
\fB\-\-test\-data\-rename\-categories\fP
.RS 4
Replace real category values with \fBvalue_1\fP, \fBvalue_2\fP, .\|.\|.
.RE
.RE
.SS "Combine and split"
.sp
\fB\-\-union\fP \fIFILE\fP \fB\-\-union\-file\fP \fIFILE\fP [\fB\-\-union\-drop\fP \fICOL\fP] [\fB\-\-union\-cast\fP \fICOL\fP=\fITYPE\fP] [\fB\-\-union\-ignore\-case\fP]
.RS 4
Stack \fIFILE\fP plus every \fB\-\-union\-file\fP into one table (at least two
files).
.sp
\fB\-\-union\-file\fP \fIFILE\fP
.RS 4
Another source. Repeatable.
.RE
.sp
\fB\-\-union\-drop\fP \fICOL\fP
.RS 4
Column to omit from the output. Repeatable.
.RE
.sp
\fB\-\-union\-cast\fP \fICOL\fP=\fITYPE\fP
.RS 4
Override a column\(cqs target Arrow type. Repeatable.
.RE
.sp
\fB\-\-union\-ignore\-case\fP
.RS 4
Treat column names differing only in case as one column. Off by
default; the first spelling encountered names the output column.
.RE
.RE
.sp
\fB\-\-join\fP \fIFILE\fP \fB\-\-join\-file\fP \fIFILE\fP \fB\-\-join\-on\fP \fICOLS\fP [\fB\-\-join\-type\fP \fITYPE\fP]
.RS 4
Join \fIFILE\fP plus every \fB\-\-join\-file\fP on the \fB\-\-join\-on\fP key(s).
.sp
\fB\-\-join\-file\fP \fIFILE\fP
.RS 4
Another source. Repeatable.
.RE
.sp
\fB\-\-join\-on\fP \fICOLS\fP
.RS 4
Key column(s), comma\-separated or repeated. Required.
.RE
.sp
\fB\-\-join\-type\fP \fITYPE\fP
.RS 4
\fBleft\fP (default), \fBinner\fP, \fBright\fP, \fBfull\fP, \fBsemi\fP (rows of the
first file with a partner), \fBanti\fP (rows without one) or \fBasof\fP (the
last \fB\-\-join\-on\fP key matches the nearest earlier value).
.RE
.RE
.sp
\fB\-\-fuzzy\-join\fP \fIFILE\fP \fB\-\-fuzzy\-join\-file\fP \fIFILE\fP \fB\-\-fuzzy\-on\fP \fILEFT\fP=\fIRIGHT\fP [\fB\-\-fuzzy\-method\fP \fINAME\fP] [\fB\-\-fuzzy\-threshold\fP \fIN\fP] [\fB\-\-fuzzy\-block\fP \fILEFT\fP=\fIRIGHT\fP] [\fB\-\-fuzzy\-join\-type\fP \fITYPE\fP] [\fB\-\-fuzzy\-max\-rows\fP \fIN\fP]
.RS 4
Join \fIFILE\fP to each \fB\-\-fuzzy\-join\-file\fP on how \fBsimilar\fP the values
are rather than on exact equality, for tables that name the same
thing differently (\f(CRMueller GmbH\fP against \f(CRMueller Gmbh.\fP). Each left
row keeps its single best partner. The output gains \fBmatch_score_N\fP
and \fBambiguous_N\fP per step; \fBambiguous\fP means the runner\-up scored
nearly as well, so that match is the one to check. The per\-step
report goes to stderr.
.sp
\fB\-\-fuzzy\-join\-file\fP \fIFILE\fP
.RS 4
The table to join against. Repeat it to join several in turn.
.RE
.sp
\fB\-\-fuzzy\-on\fP \fILEFT\fP=\fIRIGHT\fP
.RS 4
The columns to compare. May be repeated, in which case the scores
are averaged.
.RE
.sp
\fB\-\-fuzzy\-method\fP \fINAME\fP
.RS 4
\fBedit_ratio\fP (default), \fBjaro_winkler\fP or \fBtoken_set\fP.
.RE
.sp
\fB\-\-fuzzy\-threshold\fP \fIN\fP
.RS 4
The minimum average score in \fB0.0..=1.0\fP (default \fB0.85\fP).
.RE
.sp
\fB\-\-fuzzy\-block\fP \fILEFT\fP=\fIRIGHT\fP
.RS 4
Compare only rows that agree exactly on these columns, which is what
makes a large join feasible.
.RE
.sp
\fB\-\-fuzzy\-join\-type\fP \fITYPE\fP
.RS 4
\fBinner\fP, \fBleft\fP (default), \fBright\fP or \fBfull\fP.
.RE
.sp
\fB\-\-fuzzy\-max\-rows\fP \fIN\fP
.RS 4
Cap on the rows considered per side (default \fB20000\fP).
.RE
.RE
.sp
\fB\-\-spatial\-join\fP \fIFILE\fP \fB\-\-spatial\-layer\fP \fIFILE\fP.\|.\|. [\fB\-\-spatial\-op\fP \fIinside|nearest\fP] [\fB\-\-within\-km\fP \fIN\fP]
.RS 4
Join by location: each point of \fIFILE\fP gets the columns of the layer
polygon it lies in, or of the nearest layer point with its distance.
.sp
\fB\-\-spatial\-layer\fP \fIFILE\fP
.RS 4
A table to join against. Repeat it for several layers. At least one
is required.
.RE
.sp
\fB\-\-spatial\-op\fP \fIinside|nearest\fP
.RS 4
The operation. Default inside.
.RE
.sp
\fB\-\-within\-km\fP \fIN\fP
.RS 4
With \fB\-\-spatial\-op nearest\fP: points farther than this stay empty.
.RE
.RE
.sp
\fB\-\-merge\fP \fIFILE\fP \fIFILE\fP.\|.\|. [\fB\-\-merge\-original\fP \fIFILE\fP] [\fB\-\-merge\-key\fP \fICOLS\fP] [\fB\-\-merge\-prefer\fP \fIN\fP] [\fB\-\-merge\-out\fP \fIFILE\fP] [\fB\-\-merge\-format\fP \fIEXT\fP]
.RS 4
Merge two or more versions of a table, per row and per cell. With
\fB\-\-merge\-original\fP, a change made in one version is taken and only
different changes to one cell, or a row deleted in one version and
edited in another, conflict; without it, every cell where the versions
differ conflicts. Rows are matched by \fB\-\-merge\-key\fP, or by position
without one. While a conflict is open nothing is written, the
conflicts are printed on stdout and the exit code is \fB1\fP, which makes
it usable as a git merge driver: \f(CRocta \-\-merge %A %B \-\-merge\-original
%O \-\-merge\-key id \-\-merge\-out %A \-\-merge\-format %P\fP.
.sp
\fB\-\-merge\-original\fP \fIFILE\fP
.RS 4
The table every version was edited from. Optional.
.RE
.sp
\fB\-\-merge\-key\fP \fICOLS\fP
.RS 4
Key column(s), comma\-separated or repeated.
.RE
.sp
\fB\-\-merge\-prefer\fP \fIN\fP
.RS 4
Settle every conflict in favour of version \fIN\fP (1 = the first file
given to \fB\-\-merge\fP).
.RE
.sp
\fB\-\-merge\-out\fP \fIFILE\fP
.RS 4
Write the result to \fIFILE\fP instead of stdout.
.RE
.sp
\fB\-\-merge\-format\fP \fIEXT\fP
.RS 4
Format of the files, for files without an extension. An extension
(\fBcsv\fP) or a file name to take it from (git\(cqs \fB%P\fP).
.RE
.RE
.sp
\fB\-\-harmonise\-schema\fP \fIDIR\fP (\fB\-\-out\-dir\fP \fIDIR\fP | \fB\-\-combine\fP \fB\-\-out\fP \fIFILE\fP) [\fB\-\-target\-file\fP \fIFILE\fP] [\fB\-\-recursive\fP] [\fB\-\-ignore\-case\fP] [\fB\-\-overwrite\fP]
.RS 4
Rewrite every file in \fIDIR\fP to one common set of columns, writing
harmonised copies into \fB\-\-out\-dir\fP. The originals are never modified.
The target schema is the shape most files already have, or the
schema of \fB\-\-target\-file\fP when given. A column missing from a file is
added as nulls; a column not in the target is dropped and named in
the report. A file whose values cannot be cast to a target type is
\fBrefused\fP rather than written with nulls in place of those values,
because a harmonised folder of silently emptied cells looks clean and
is not. Two inputs that would write the same output name are both
refused rather than one being renamed. The report goes to stdout;
refusals and dropped columns go to stderr. Exit code is \fB0\fP when
nothing was refused and \fB1\fP otherwise. Add \fB\-\-recursive\fP,
\fB\-\-ignore\-case\fP, and \fB\-\-overwrite\fP to replace existing files in the
output folder.
.sp
\fB\-\-target\-file\fP \fIFILE\fP
.RS 4
Take the target schema from this file instead of the shape most
files already have.
.RE
.sp
\fB\-\-combine\fP
.RS 4
Fold the folder into ONE file, written to \fB\-\-out\fP \fIFILE\fP, instead of
writing a harmonised copy of each. A \fBsource_file\fP column says which
file every row came from (suffixed, as \fBsource_file_2\fP, if the data
already has one). Nothing is cast, so nothing is refused: columns
only some files have are kept and left empty for the others, as
\fB\-\-union\fP does. A file that cannot be read is named, skipped and
counted. Asking for it without \fB\-\-out\fP is refused rather than guessed
at.
.RE
.RE
.sp
\fB\-\-partition\-by\fP \fICOL\fP \fB\-\-out\-dir\fP \fIDIR\fP \fIFILE\fP [\fB\-\-partition\-format\fP \fIEXT\fP] [\fB\-\-partition\-layout\fP \fILAYOUT\fP]
.RS 4
Split \fIFILE\fP into one file per distinct value of \fICOL\fP, written into
\fB\-\-out\-dir\fP. Combines with \fB\-\-split\-rows\fP / \fB\-\-split\-size\fP to cap
each piece as well.
.sp
\fB\-\-partition\-format\fP \fIEXT\fP
.RS 4
Output extension (default: the source\(cqs).
.RE
.sp
\fB\-\-partition\-layout\fP \fILAYOUT\fP
.RS 4
How the pieces are named. \f(CRflat\fP (default) writes \f(CR<value>.<ext>\fP
side by side; \f(CRfolder\fP writes \f(CR<value>/part\-0001.<ext>\fP; \f(CRhive\fP
writes \f(CR<column>=<value>/data.<ext>\fP; \f(CRhive\-parts\fP writes
\f(CR<column>=<value>/part\-0001.<ext>\fP. All four hold the same rows and
all four reopen as one table, since the partition column is written
into every file. Only \f(CRflat\fP loses information in the name: it
sanitises and lowercases the value into a file stem, so values
differing only in case or punctuation collide and gain \f(CR_2\fP, \f(CR_3\fP.
The folder layouts keep the value as it is, replacing only
characters that cannot appear in a path. \f(CRhive\fP is what Spark,
Athena, DuckDB and pandas expect from a partitioned dataset.
.RE
.RE
.sp
\fB\-\-split\-rows\fP \fIN\fP | \fB\-\-split\-size\fP \fISIZE\fP \fB\-\-out\-dir\fP \fIDIR\fP \fIFILE\fP [\fB\-\-partition\-by\fP \fICOL\fP]
.RS 4
Split \fIFILE\fP into pieces of at most \fIN\fP rows or \fISIZE\fP bytes each,
written into \fB\-\-out\-dir\fP. Works alone (splits the whole table) or with
\fB\-\-partition\-by\fP (splits each group). Every file then gets a part
number: \f(CRname_part_0001.ext\fP, or \f(CRpart\-0001.ext\fP inside a folder.
.sp
\fB\-\-split\-rows\fP \fIN\fP
.RS 4
At most \fIN\fP rows per output file.
.RE
.sp
\fB\-\-split\-size\fP \fISIZE\fP
.RS 4
At most \fISIZE\fP per output file, e.g. \f(CR25MB\fP, \f(CR500KB\fP, \f(CR1GB\fP (1 MB =
1,000,000 bytes; \f(CRMiB\fP for 1,048,576). Combines with \fB\-\-split\-rows\fP.
Files land a little under the limit.
.RE
.RE
.sp
\fB\-\-to\-workbook\fP \fIOUT.xlsx\fP \fIFILE\fP.\|.\|.
.RS 4
Write every \fIFILE\fP into one Excel workbook, one worksheet per file.
Sheet names come from the file stems and are corrected to Excel\(cqs
rules automatically: at most 31 characters, no forbidden punctuation,
and duplicates numbered \f(CR_2\fP, \f(CR_3\fP. Needs at least two inputs; for a
single file use \fB\-\-convert\fP. Prints a headerless
\f(CRinput<TAB>sheet<TAB>rows\fP listing. Every input is read before
anything is written, so an unreadable file fails the run instead of
leaving a half\-written workbook.
.RE
.SS "Convert and SQL"
.sp
\fB\-\-convert\fP \fIIN\fP \fIOUT\fP [\fB\-\-sort\-by\fP \fICOLS\fP] [\fB\-\-password\-env\fP \fIVAR\fP] [\fB\-\-compression\fP \fICODEC\fP] [\fB\-\-row\-group\-size\fP \fIN\fP]
.RS 4
Convert \fIIN\fP to \fIOUT\fP. Both formats are inferred from each path\(cqs
extension and routed through the shared format registry. Read\-only
output formats (SAS, R datasets, HDF5, NetCDF, EPUB, GeoJSON) are
rejected with a clear error. Conversion is bounded by the
initial\-load cap (2,000,000 rows by default); pass \fB\-\-rows all\fP to
convert the full file. \fIOUT\fP may be \fB\-\fP for standard output, with
\fB\-\-to\fP naming the format, since a pipe has no name.
.RE
.sp
\fB\-\-batch\-convert\fP \fIFILE\fP.\|.\|. \fB\-\-out\-dir\fP \fIDIR\fP \fB\-\-to\fP \fIEXT\fP [\fB\-\-overwrite\fP] [\fB\-\-password\-env\fP \fIVAR\fP]
.RS 4
Convert every \fIFILE\fP into \fB\-\-out\-dir\fP as \fB\-\-to\fP \fIEXT\fP. Output names
are \f(CR<stem>.<ext>\fP; two inputs sharing a stem get \f(CR_2\fP, \f(CR_3\fP suffixes
in input order, so a run cannot overwrite its own earlier output.
Existing outputs are skipped unless \fB\-\-overwrite\fP is given. One failed
file does not stop the run. Output is a headerless
\f(CRinput<TAB>output<TAB>status\fP listing on stdout, with per\-file errors
and a summary on stderr. The exit code is \fB1\fP when any file failed.
.RE
.sp
\fB\-\-sql\fP \fIFILE\fP \fB\-q\fP \fIQUERY\fP [\fB\-\-sql\-table\fP \fINAME\fP=\fIPATH\fP .\|.\|.] [\fB\-\-sql\-attach\fP \fIALIAS\fP=\fIPATH\fP .\|.\|.] [\fB\-\-sql\-write\-to\fP \fIPATH\fP \fB\-\-sql\-write\-table\fP \fITABLE\fP [\fB\-\-sql\-write\-schema\fP \fISCHEMA\fP] [\fB\-\-sql\-write\-mode\fP \fIMODE\fP]] [\fB\-\-stream\fP] [\fB\-\-explain\fP]
.RS 4
Run a SQL query against \fIFILE\fP, which DuckDB sees as a table called
\fBdata\fP. Extra tables and whole DuckDB / SQLite databases can be added,
so a single invocation can JOIN across formats, and the SELECT result
can be persisted to a DuckDB or SQLite file. Mutations (INSERT /
UPDATE / DELETE) on \fBdata\fP itself do \fBnot\fP persist back to \fIFILE\fP,
since the in\-memory DuckDB connection is discarded at exit. Use
\fB\-\-convert\fP on the post\-mutation output if you need to persist.
.sp
\fB\-q\fP \fIQUERY\fP, \fB\-\-query\fP \fIQUERY\fP
.RS 4
The SQL query. Always reference the file\(cqs data as the table \fBdata\fP.
.RE
.sp
\fB\-\-sql\-table\fP \fINAME\fP=\fIPATH\fP
.RS 4
Register an extra file as a workspace table named \fINAME\fP. Any
supported format. Repeatable.
.RE
.sp
\fB\-\-sql\-attach\fP \fIALIAS\fP=\fIPATH\fP
.RS 4
ATTACH a DuckDB or SQLite database under \fIALIAS\fP. Repeatable. After
attachment every inner table is queryable as \fBalias.schema.tbl\fP
(DuckDB) or \fBalias.tbl\fP (SQLite when the DuckDB sqlite extension is
bundled, otherwise per\-table fallback registration under
\fBalias__table\fP).
.RE
.sp
\fB\-\-sql\-write\-to\fP \fIPATH\fP
.RS 4
Persist the SELECT result to \fIPATH\fP instead of printing it. The
extension picks DuckDB (\fB.duckdb\fP, \fB.ddb\fP) or SQLite (everything
else). Created if missing. Requires \fB\-\-sql\-write\-table\fP.
.RE
.sp
\fB\-\-sql\-write\-table\fP \fITABLE\fP
.RS 4
Target table name for \fB\-\-sql\-write\-to\fP.
.RE
.sp
\fB\-\-sql\-write\-schema\fP \fISCHEMA\fP
.RS 4
Target schema for \fB\-\-sql\-write\-to\fP. DuckDB only; defaults to \fBmain\fP.
SQLite has no schemas; pass \fBmain\fP or leave unset.
.RE
.sp
\fB\-\-sql\-write\-mode\fP \fIMODE\fP
.RS 4
\fBcreate\fP (default; errors if the table already exists), \fBreplace\fP
(drop and recreate), or \fBappend\fP (INSERT into the existing table;
column count and order must match).
.RE
.sp
\fB\-\-stream\fP
.RS 4
Let DuckDB scan the file where it lies instead of loading its rows.
For a Parquet, CSV or JSON file this lets an aggregate cover every
row of a file far larger than memory: the primary file is
registered as a view named \f(CRdata\fP rather than as a loaded table, so
\fB\-\-rows\fP no longer bounds what the query sees. Every other action
needs the rows themselves and says so on stderr rather than ignoring
the flag. A format DuckDB cannot scan also falls back to a normal
read with a note.
.RE
.sp
\fB\-\-explain\fP
.RS 4
Show the query plan instead of running the statement. Prints the
steps in plain words, numbered in the order the database works
through them; with \fB\-f json\fP or \fB\-f csv\fP, one row per step. One
statement. The plan is DuckDB\(cqs own (the local workspace). Nothing
is executed. Also works with \fB\-\-db\-query\fP.
.RE
.RE
.sp
\fB\-\-export\-schema\fP \fIFILE\fP [\fB\-t\fP \fITARGET\fP]
.RS 4
Render \fIFILE\fP\*(Aqs column schema as SQL DDL, a Pydantic model, a
TypeScript interface, a JSON Schema document, or a Rust struct, and
print it to standard output. Only the column list is read and no
rows are serialised. Short form: \fB\-e\fP.
.sp
\fB\-t\fP \fITARGET\fP, \fB\-\-target\fP \fITARGET\fP
.RS 4
One of \fBpostgres\fP (default), \fBmysql\fP, \fBsqlite\fP, \fBdatabricks\fP,
\fBsnowflake\fP, \fBpydantic\fP, \fBtypescript\fP, \fBjson\-schema\fP, or \fBrust\fP.
.RE
.RE
.SS "Databases"
.sp
These actions work on a saved database connection, managed in
Settings \(-> Databases or with \fB\-\-add\-connection\fP. Two options are shared
by the group:
.sp
\fB\-\-db\fP \fICONNECTION\fP
.RS 4
Saved connection name (case\-insensitive) or id. Passwords stay in the
system keyring.
.RE
.sp
\fB\-\-db\-catalog\fP \fINAME\fP
.RS 4
Catalog (the top namespace level) for a three\-level engine, used by
\fB\-\-db\-tables\fP, \fB\-\-db\-write\-table\fP and \fB\-\-db\-export\fP. Only Snowflake,
Databricks and BigQuery have one; passing it to any other engine is an
error. With \fB\-\-db\-tables\fP and no catalog, the catalogs themselves are
listed.
.RE
.sp
\fB\-\-db\-tables\fP \fB\-\-db\fP \fICONNECTION\fP [\fB\-\-db\-catalog\fP \fINAME\fP]
.RS 4
List every schema and table of the connection as a two\-column table.
On Trino, Snowflake, Databricks and BigQuery, which have a catalog
level above the schema, the catalogs themselves are listed unless
\fB\-\-db\-catalog\fP names one.
.RE
.sp
\fB\-\-db\-query\fP \fISQL\fP \fB\-\-db\fP \fICONNECTION\fP [\fB\-\-explain\fP]
.RS 4
Run one SQL statement on the connection, server\-side and in the
engine\(cqs native dialect. SELECT results print in \fB\-f\fP \fIFORMAT\fP;
mutations report rows affected and are refused unless the
connection\(cqs "Allow writes" switch is on. Results stop at the
initial\-load row cap; use \fB\-\-rows\fP to raise or lift it.
.sp
\fB\-\-explain\fP
.RS 4
Show the query plan instead of running the statement, as for
\fB\-\-sql\fP. Works on PostgreSQL, MySQL / MariaDB, Databricks and
BigQuery (BigQuery: the bytes it would read). Nothing is executed.
.RE
.RE
.sp
\fB\-\-db\-write\-table\fP \fISCHEMA\fP.\fITABLE\fP \fB\-\-db\fP \fICONNECTION\fP \fIFILE\fP [\fB\-\-db\-write\-mode\fP \fIMODE\fP] [\fB\-\-db\-catalog\fP \fINAME\fP]
.RS 4
Write \fIFILE\fP into the given server table. Refused unless the
connection allows writes.
.sp
\fB\-\-db\-write\-mode\fP \fIMODE\fP
.RS 4
\fBcreate\fP (default; error if the table exists), \fBappend\fP, or
\fBreplace\fP.
.RE
.RE
.sp
\fB\-\-db\-copy\fP \fISCHEMA\fP.\fITABLE\fP \fB\-\-db\fP \fICONNECTION\fP \fB\-\-db\-copy\-to\fP \fICONNECTION\fP [\fB\-\-db\-copy\-target\fP \fISCHEMA\fP.\fITABLE\fP] [\fB\-\-db\-copy\-target\-catalog\fP \fINAME\fP] [\fB\-\-db\-write\-mode\fP \fIMODE\fP]
.RS 4
Copy a table from the \fB\-\-db\fP connection to another saved connection,
server to server. Refused unless the target connection allows writes.
.sp
\fB\-\-db\-copy\-to\fP \fICONNECTION\fP
.RS 4
Target connection, a saved connection name or id. Required.
.RE
.sp
\fB\-\-db\-copy\-target\fP \fISCHEMA\fP.\fITABLE\fP
.RS 4
Target table. Defaults to the source schema and table.
.RE
.sp
\fB\-\-db\-copy\-target\-catalog\fP \fINAME\fP
.RS 4
Target catalog on a three\-level engine.
.RE
.sp
\fB\-\-db\-write\-mode\fP \fIMODE\fP
.RS 4
\fBcreate\fP (default), \fBappend\fP or \fBreplace\fP.
.RE
.RE
.sp
\fB\-\-db\-export\fP \fISCHEMA\fP \fB\-\-db\fP \fICONNECTION\fP \fB\-\-out\-dir\fP \fIDIR\fP [\fB\-\-to\fP \fIEXT\fP] [\fB\-\-db\-export\-tables\fP \fIT1\fP,\fIT2\fP] [\fB\-\-overwrite\fP]
.RS 4
Export the tables of \fISCHEMA\fP into \fB\-\-out\-dir\fP, one file per table
named after it. \fB\-\-to\fP picks \fBparquet\fP (default), \fBcsv\fP, \fBtsv\fP or
\fBjsonl\fP. Each table is read page by page, so its size does not
matter. A file that is already there is skipped unless \fB\-\-overwrite\fP.
One failed table does not stop the rest; the exit code is 1 if any
failed. On a three\-level engine name the catalog with \fB\-\-db\-catalog\fP.
.sp
\fB\-\-db\-export\-tables\fP \fIT1\fP,\fIT2\fP
.RS 4
Tables to export, comma separated. Default: every table of the
schema.
.RE
.RE
.sp
\fB\-\-sync\-sql\fP \fIFILE\fP \fB\-\-db\fP \fICONNECTION\fP \fB\-\-sync\-table\fP \fISCHEMA.TABLE\fP \fB\-\-sync\-on\fP \fICOLS\fP
.RS 4
Print the SQL that would make a live database table match \fIFILE\fP, and
write nothing. Reads the table, matches rows on \fB\-\-sync\-on\fP, and
prints one transaction to stdout: DELETEs for rows only on the
server, full\-row UPDATEs for rows whose values differ, INSERTs for
rows only in the file. Counts go to stderr, so the script itself
pipes cleanly to a file or to \fBpsql\fP. For teams whose database changes
must be reviewed as a script rather than applied by a tool.
.sp
Numbers are compared as numbers, so a file\(cqs \f(CR120.50\fP and a
\f(CRnumeric(12,2)\fP column\(cqs \f(CR120.50\fP are not a change. Columns present only
in the file are reported on stderr and skipped: this never emits \fBALTER
TABLE\fP. Only the rows loaded under the initial\-load cap are compared;
use \fB\-\-rows\fP to raise it.
.sp
\fB\-\-sync\-table\fP \fISCHEMA.TABLE\fP
.RS 4
Target table. A three\-part \fICATALOG.SCHEMA.TABLE\fP is accepted for
the engines that have catalogs. Required.
.RE
.sp
\fB\-\-sync\-on\fP \fICOLS\fP
.RS 4
Comma\-separated key columns matching file rows to table rows.
Required: without a key there is no way to tell an updated row from
a delete plus an insert.
.RE
.RE
.SS "Cloud and web"
.sp
The cloud actions take \fBs3://\fP, \fBaz://\fP, \fBgs://\fP, \fBsftp://\fP and \fBdavs://\fP
URLs. Credentials come from a saved connection covering the URL, else
the ambient chain.
.sp
\fB\-\-cloud\-ls\fP \fIURL\fP [\fB\-\-recursive\fP]
.RS 4
List a cloud bucket or prefix, one folder level by default. Add
\fB\-\-recursive\fP to flatten everything under the prefix.
.RE
.sp
\fB\-\-cloud\-get\fP \fIURL\fP \fB\-\-out\fP \fIFILE\fP
.RS 4
Download one cloud object to \fIFILE\fP.
.RE
.sp
\fB\-\-cloud\-put\fP \fIFILE\fP \fB\-\-to\fP \fIURL\fP
.RS 4
Upload a local file to the cloud URL.
.RE
.sp
\fB\-\-cloud\-copy\fP \fIURL\fP \fB\-\-to\fP \fIURL\fP
.RS 4
Copy a cloud object, or a whole prefix (source ending in \f(CR/\fP), to the
\fB\-\-to\fP URL. Within one bucket the backend copies server\-side; across
buckets, accounts or providers the object is streamed in blocks, so
object size does not drive memory. At most 10,000 objects per run.
.RE
.sp
\fB\-\-cloud\-move\fP \fIURL\fP \fB\-\-to\fP \fIURL\fP
.RS 4
Like \fB\-\-cloud\-copy\fP, then delete the source. Object stores have no
rename. The delete runs only after every copy succeeded.
.RE
.sp
\fB\-\-cloud\-delete\fP \fIURL\fP [\fB\-\-recursive\fP]
.RS 4
Delete a cloud object, or a whole prefix with \fB\-\-recursive\fP. Cannot be
undone unless the bucket has versioning enabled.
.RE
.sp
\fB\-\-api\fP \fICONNECTION\fP [\fB\-\-api\-path\fP \fIPATH\fP]
.RS 4
Read a saved REST/JSON API endpoint (Settings \(-> API endpoints) as a
table. It takes the connection\(cqs name or id, never a URL: the host and
its credential were chosen when the connection was saved. Its
authentication is applied and its pagination is walked, so the result
is every page\(cqs rows. Reading stops at the endpoint\(cqs last page, at
the row cap (\fB\-\-rows\fP), or at a fixed page ceiling; stopping on a
limit is noted on stderr. A next\-page link pointing at a different
host stops the read with an error rather than being followed. For a
one\-off address, give any read action an \fBhttps://\fP URL instead.
.sp
\fB\-\-api\-path\fP \fIPATH\fP
.RS 4
Path under the endpoint\(cqs base URL, overriding the connection\(cqs own.
It is always joined under that base URL, so it cannot move the
request to another host.
.RE
.RE
.SS "Connections"
.sp
\fB\-\-list\-connections\fP
.RS 4
Print the saved cloud and database connections (names and targets
only, never secrets).
.RE
.sp
\fB\-\-add\-connection\fP \fISPEC\fP [\fB\-\-secret\-env\fP \fIVAR\fP]
.RS 4
Add or update a saved connection from a \fBkey=value,key=value\fP spec.
\fBkind=\fP and \fBname=\fP are required. A connection with the same name is
replaced wholesale, keeping its id and stored secret, so re\-running a
provisioning script is idempotent; omitted keys revert to defaults.
Unknown keys are an error.
Cloud (\fBkind=s3|azure|gcs\fP): bucket, region, endpoint, prefix,
account, profile, account_level, anonymous, allow_writes,
force_path_style, allow_http.
Database (\fBkind=postgres|mysql|mssql|oracle|redshift|clickhouse|exasol|
trino|athena|snowflake|databricks|bigquery\fP): host, port, database,
user, allow_writes, query_timeout. \fBquery_timeout\fP is whole seconds
(default 60) and is read only by the engines that poll over HTTP for
their results (Trino, Athena, Snowflake, Databricks, BigQuery).
PostgreSQL, Redshift and MySQL also
take sslmode (disable|prefer|require|verify\-ca|verify\-full, or MySQL\(cqs
disabled|preferred|required|verify_identity; default prefer),
sslrootcert (a PEM CA file) and hostaddr (an IP address to dial
instead of host, which then only names the certificate).
File database (\fBkind=duckdb|sqlite\fP): file (the path, which must
exist) and allow_writes; other keys and \fB\-\-secret\-env\fP are refused.
Only password authentication can be expressed here; other methods need
the Settings dialog.
.sp
\fB\-\-secret\-env\fP \fIVAR\fP
.RS 4
Name of an environment variable holding the secret, so it never
appears in argv. Database: the password. S3:
\fIACCESS_KEY_ID:SECRET_ACCESS_KEY[:TOKEN]\fP. Azure: the account key or
a SAS token. GCS uses application\-default credentials and takes no
secret.
.RE
.RE
.sp
\fB\-\-remove\-connection\fP \fINAME\fP
.RS 4
Remove a saved connection by name or id, and delete its stored secret.
.RE
.SS "MCP server"
.sp
\fB\-\-mcp\fP [\fB\-\-mcp\-read\-only\fP] [\fB\-\-mcp\-tools\fP \fILIST\fP] [\fB\-\-mcp\-without\fP \fILIST\fP]
.RS 4
Start a Model Context Protocol (MCP) server that speaks JSON\-RPC on
standard input / output, so an AI agent can read, query, compare and
(unless read\-only) write tabular data through Octa. The full tool list
with descriptions is what the server returns for \fBtools/list\fP; the
online documentation has a page per tool. The tools come in groups:
\fBcore\fP (reading, describing, searching and SQL), \fBquality\fP
(duplicates, outliers, PII, rules and correlations), \fBcompare\fP
(schemas, row\-level differences and drift between two sources),
\fBcombine\fP (unions, joins, join keys and reconciling column names),
\fBreshape\fP (pivot, resample, rolling windows, dedupe, impute,
anonymise), \fBdatabases\fP (live database connections, their tables and
queries), \fBcloud\fP (objects in S3, Azure and GCS) and \fBwrite\fP (writing
files, editing tables, converting, charts and reports).
.sp
The defaults (response row cap of 1000 rows, per\-cell byte cap of 64
KiB, and file\-loader cap of 2,000,000 rows) are configurable under
Octa\(cqs Settings \(-> MCP and Settings \(-> Performance. They are read once
at server startup; changes require a restart. Per call, pass \fBlimit: 0\fP
to lift the response cap and \fBunlimited: true\fP to lift the file\-loader
cap so the tool sees every row on disk. Parquet files with very many
row groups fall back to a DuckDB\-backed reader automatically.
.sp
\fB\-\-mcp\-read\-only\fP
.RS 4
Leave out every tool that writes: the \fBwrite\fP group, the \fBcloud\fP
tools other than the listing, and the two database writers. The
server then exposes a read\-only surface.
.RE
.sp
\fB\-\-mcp\-tools\fP=\fILIST\fP
.RS 4
Advertise ONLY these tools, comma\-separated. Takes group names
(above) and individual tool names, mixed freely:
\fB\-\-mcp\-tools core,databases\fP or \fB\-\-mcp\-tools read_table,run_sql\fP.
Without it every tool is advertised. A tool left out is also not
callable. An unrecognised name is an error and the server does not
start. This is the lever for an agent\(cqs context size: the client
reads the tool list once and carries it in every request to its
model, and all of Octa\(cqs tools together are roughly 39,000 tokens of
description and schema.
.RE
.sp
\fB\-\-mcp\-without\fP=\fILIST\fP
.RS 4
Advertise everything except these, in the same spelling as
\fB\-\-mcp\-tools\fP. Applied after it, so the two combine:
\fB\-\-mcp\-tools core,write \-\-mcp\-without convert\fP.
.RE
.RE
.SH "COMMON OPTIONS"
.sp
\fB\-f\fP \fIFORMAT\fP, \fB\-\-format\fP \fIFORMAT\fP
.RS 4
Output format for every action that prints a table. It has no effect
for \fB\-\-convert\fP (whose output format comes from the output path\(cqs
extension), \fB\-\-export\-schema\fP (which emits source code chosen by
\fB\-t\fP), the plain listings \fB\-\-partition\-by\fP, \fB\-\-batch\-convert\fP and
\fB\-\-to\-workbook\fP print, or \fB\-\-mcp\fP. \fIFORMAT\fP is one of:
.sp
\fBtsv\fP (default)
.RS 4
Tab\-separated values, one row per line, header row first. TAB and
newline characters in cells are replaced with spaces, since TSV has
no escape mechanism.
.RE
.sp
\fBjson\fP
.RS 4
Pretty\-printed JSON array of \f(CR{column: value}\fP objects. Numeric
and boolean cells keep their native JSON types; dates, blobs and
nested values become strings.
.RE
.sp
\fBcsv\fP
.RS 4
RFC 4180 CSV. Fields with comma, quote, or newline are properly
quoted; embedded quotes are doubled.
.RE
.RE
.sp
\fB\-\-rows\fP \fIN\fP|\fBall\fP
.RS 4
Override the initial\-load row cap for this invocation. Streaming
formats (Parquet, CSV, TSV) honour a process\-wide cap (default
\fB2,000,000\fP rows). Pass a number (commas/underscores allowed) to
raise it or \fBall\fP to disable the cap entirely. Applies to every action
that reads a file. \fB\-\-partition\-by\fP, \fB\-\-split\-rows\fP and \fB\-\-split\-size\fP
read every row unless \fB\-\-rows\fP says otherwise, so no piece is silently
missing.
.RE
.sp
\fB\-n\fP \fIN\fP, \fB\-\-lines\fP \fIN\fP
.RS 4
Row count for \fB\-\-head\fP, \fB\-\-tail\fP, and \fB\-\-sample\fP. Default \fB20\fP.
.RE
.sp
\fB\-\-seed\fP \fIN\fP
.RS 4
Seed for \fB\-\-sample\fP and \fB\-\-test\-data\fP, for reproducible output.
Default \fB0\fP.
.RE
.sp
\fB\-\-sort\-by\fP \fICOLS\fP
.RS 4
Sort the rows of \fB\-\-head\fP or \fB\-\-convert\fP by these columns, first key
first (comma\-separated or repeated). Sorts the rows that were read, up
to the row cap: add \fB\-\-rows all\fP to sort a whole large file. With
\fB\-\-head\fP the sort happens before the first \fIN\fP rows are taken.
.RE
.sp
\fB\-\-sort\-desc\fP \fICOLS\fP
.RS 4
The \fB\-\-sort\-by\fP columns that sort high to low.
.RE
.sp
\fB\-\-natural\fP
.RS 4
Sort text naturally (file2 before file10) instead of letter by letter,
the default.
.RE
.sp
\fB\-\-table\fP \fINAME\fP
.RS 4
For \fB\-\-describe\fP, \fB\-\-validate\-schema\fP, \fB\-\-unique\-columns\fP: the
table to read from \fIFILE\fP when the source is multi\-table.
.RE
.sp
\fB\-\-table\-a\fP \fINAME\fP, \fB\-\-table\-b\fP \fINAME\fP
.RS 4
For \fB\-\-compare\-schemas\fP, \fB\-\-compare\-distributions\fP and
\fB\-\-check\-references\fP: the table to read from each side when the
source is multi\-table (SQLite, DuckDB, GeoPackage).
.RE
.sp
\fB\-\-out\-dir\fP \fIDIR\fP
.RS 4
Output directory for \fB\-\-partition\-by\fP, \fB\-\-split\-rows\fP, \fB\-\-split\-size\fP,
\fB\-\-batch\-convert\fP, \fB\-\-harmonise\-schema\fP and \fB\-\-db\-export\fP (required;
created if absent).
.RE
.sp
\fB\-\-out\fP \fIFILE\fP
.RS 4
Output file for \fB\-\-cloud\-get\fP and for \fB\-\-harmonise\-schema \-\-combine\fP.
.RE
.sp
\fB\-\-to\fP \fIEXT\fP|\fIURL\fP
.RS 4
The target extension for \fB\-\-convert IN \-\fP (no leading dot, e.g. \f(CRcsv\fP,
\f(CRparquet\fP, \f(CRjson\fP), \fB\-\-batch\-convert\fP and \fB\-\-db\-export\fP; the cloud URL
for \fB\-\-cloud\-put\fP, \fB\-\-cloud\-copy\fP and \fB\-\-cloud\-move\fP.
.RE
.sp
\fB\-\-overwrite\fP
.RS 4
Replace existing outputs in \fB\-\-batch\-convert\fP, \fB\-\-harmonise\-schema\fP and
\fB\-\-db\-export\fP, which skip them otherwise.
.RE
.sp
\fB\-\-recursive\fP
.RS 4
Walk subdirectories for \fB\-\-schema\-drift\fP, \fB\-\-relationships\fP and
\fB\-\-harmonise\-schema\fP. For \fB\-\-cloud\-ls\fP it flattens the listing; for
\fB\-\-cloud\-delete\fP it is the required confirmation that a folder delete
is meant.
.RE
.sp
\fB\-\-ignore\-case\fP
.RS 4
Treat column names differing only in case as the same column
(\fB\-\-schema\-drift\fP, \fB\-\-harmonise\-schema\fP).
.RE
.sp
\fB\-\-password\-env\fP \fIVAR\fP
.RS 4
With \fB\-\-convert\fP, write only a password\-protected AES\-256 zip
(\f(CRout.csv\fP becomes \f(CRout.csv.zip\fP); with \fB\-\-batch\-convert\fP, write every
output as its own zip (\f(CRa.csv\fP becomes \f(CRa.csv.zip\fP). The passphrase is
taken from environment variable \fIVAR\fP, never from argv.
.RE
.sp
\fB\-\-compression\fP \fICODEC\fP
.RS 4
Compression codec for the written file: \fBuncompressed\fP, \fBsnappy\fP,
\fBzstd\fP, \fBgzip\fP or \fBlz4\fP. Applies to \fB\-\-convert\fP and
\fB\-\-batch\-convert\fP; Parquet targets only, ignored by other formats.
An unknown name is rejected before any file is written. Omitted, the
codec saved in \fBSettings \(-> Files \(-> Write options\fP is used, or \fBzstd\fP
when nothing is saved.
.RE
.sp
\fB\-\-row\-group\-size\fP \fIN\fP
.RS 4
Rows per Parquet row group for \fB\-\-convert\fP and \fB\-\-batch\-convert\fP.
Larger groups scan faster; smaller groups let readers skip more
precisely. Omitted means the writer\(cqs own default.
.RE
.sp
\fB\-\-completions\fP \fISHELL\fP
.RS 4
Print a shell completion script for \fISHELL\fP to standard output and exit.
One of \fBbash\fP, \fBzsh\fP, \fBfish\fP, \fBpowershell\fP, \fBelvish\fP. The script is
generated from Octa\(cqs own argument list, so it completes every flag this
page documents. Wire it into the running shell with
\f(CReval "$(octa \-\-completions zsh)"\fP, or let \fBinstall.sh\fP write the files
under the install prefix.
.RE
.sp
\fB\-h\fP, \fB\-\-help\fP
.RS 4
Print the flag list and exit. \fB\-h\fP and \fB\-\-help\fP produce the same
output.
.RE
.sp
\fB\-\-help\-all\fP
.RS 4
Print the flag list plus worked examples for every action, and exit.
The examples are behind their own flag because printing them by
default buried the flag list under something the size of this manual
page.
.RE
.sp
\fB\-\-version\fP
.RS 4
Print the Octa version and exit.
.RE
.SH "OUTPUT"
.sp
Tabular data is written to \fBstandard output\fP. Status messages,
warnings, and errors are written to \fBstandard error\fP. This means
\fBocta \-\-sql FILE \-q QUERY \-f json | jq .\|.\|.\fP is safe even when an
error occurs, since the data stream stays clean.
.sp
\fB\-\-batch\-convert\fP, \fB\-\-harmonise\-schema\fP, \fB\-\-partition\-by\fP, \fB\-\-db\-copy\fP
and \fB\-\-db\-export\fP report progress on stderr while they run, on a single
line that rewrites itself (\f(CR[137/500] file.csv  ETA 1:12\fP). The line
appears only when stderr is a terminal, so redirected output and CI logs
are unchanged.
.SH "EXIT STATUS"
.sp
\fB0\fP
.RS 4
Success.
.RE
.sp
\fB1\fP
.RS 4
Any error: invalid arguments, file not found, parse failure, write
rejection, and so on. These actions also exit \fB1\fP on a finding, so CI
pipelines can gate on them directly:
.sp
\fB\-\-validate\-schema\fP
.RS 4
The schemas differ.
.RE
.sp
\fB\-\-schema\-drift\fP
.RS 4
The files disagree about their columns.
.RE
.sp
\fB\-\-check\fP
.RS 4
A rule failed or could not run.
.RE
.sp
\fB\-\-check\-references\fP
.RS 4
Child rows have no parent.
.RE
.sp
\fB\-\-compare\-folders\fP
.RS 4
A file differs or is missing, or a folder hit the file limit.
.RE
.sp
\fB\-\-drift\-report\fP
.RS 4
A \fB\-\-fail\-on\fP gate was breached.
.RE
.sp
\fB\-\-overlaps\fP
.RS 4
Overlapping spans were found.
.RE
.sp
\fB\-\-merge\fP
.RS 4
A conflict is open.
.RE
.sp
\fB\-\-recipe\fP
.RS 4
A step could not run.
.RE
.sp
\fB\-\-harmonise\-schema\fP
.RS 4
A file was refused.
.RE
.sp
\fB\-\-batch\-convert\fP
.RS 4
An input failed.
.RE
.sp
\fB\-\-db\-export\fP
.RS 4
A table failed.
.RE
.RE
.SH "EXAMPLES"
.SS "Look at a file"
.sp
Open multiple files in the GUI
.RS 4
.sp
.if n .RS 4
.nf
.fam C
octa file1.csv file2.parquet file3.json
.fam
.fi
.if n .RE
.RE
.sp
Print the schema of a Parquet file
.RS 4
.sp
.if n .RS 4
.nf
.fam C
octa \-\-schema sales.parquet
.fam
.fi
.if n .RE
.RE
.sp
Print the first 5 rows of a CSV as JSON
.RS 4
.sp
.if n .RS 4
.nf
.fam C
octa \-\-head data.csv \-n 5 \-f json
.fam
.fi
.if n .RE
.RE
.sp
Print the last rows / a reproducible random sample
.RS 4
.sp
.if n .RS 4
.nf
.fam C
octa \-\-tail data.csv \-n 5
octa \-\-sample data.parquet \-n 20 \-\-seed 1
.fam
.fi
.if n .RE
.RE
.sp
One\-shot file snapshot
.RS 4
.sp
.if n .RS 4
.nf
.fam C
octa \-\-describe data.parquet \-\-sample\-rows 3
octa \-\-describe users.sqlite \-\-table customers \-f json
.fam
.fi
.if n .RE
.RE
.SS "Analyse"
.sp
Find primary\-key candidates
.RS 4
.sp
.if n .RS 4
.nf
.fam C
octa \-\-unique\-columns users.csv
octa \-\-unique\-columns orders.parquet \-\-max\-combo 2 \-f json
.fam
.fi
.if n .RE
.RE
.sp
Did a column\(cqs distribution move between two files?
.RS 4
.sp
.if n .RS 4
.nf
.fam C
octa \-\-compare\-distributions sales.csv \-\-dist\-column amount \-\-dist\-column\-b amount_last_year
octa \-\-compare\-distributions q2.parquet \-\-dist\-column price \-\-dist\-file\-b q3.parquet
.fam
.fi
.if n .RE
.RE
.SS "Compare and check"
.sp
Diff two schemas
.RS 4
.sp
.if n .RS 4
.nf
.fam C
octa \-\-compare\-schemas v1.parquet v2.parquet
octa \-\-compare\-schemas a.sqlite b.sqlite \-\-table\-a users \-\-table\-b users \-f json
.fam
.fi
.if n .RE
.RE
.sp
Diff two files\*(Aq rows
.RS 4
.sp
.if n .RS 4
.nf
.fam C
octa \-\-diff v1.csv v2.csv
octa \-\-diff v1.csv v2.csv \-\-diff\-mode ordered
octa \-\-diff v1.csv v2.csv \-\-diff\-mode join \-\-diff\-on id
octa \-\-diff a.parquet b.parquet \-f json
.fam
.fi
.if n .RE
.RE
.sp
Validate a file against a JSON Schema in CI
.RS 4
.sp
.if n .RS 4
.nf
.fam C
octa \-\-export\-schema sales.parquet \-t json\-schema > sales.schema.json
octa \-\-validate\-schema sales.parquet \-\-expect\-schema sales.schema.json
.fam
.fi
.if n .RE
.RE
.sp
Find orders whose customer does not exist
.RS 4
.sp
.if n .RS 4
.nf
.fam C
octa \-\-check\-references customers.csv \-\-parent\-column id \(rs
     \-\-child\-file orders.csv \-\-child\-column customer_id
.fam
.fi
.if n .RE
.RE
.SS "Clean and transform"
.sp
Add a stable key built from several columns
.RS 4
.sp
.if n .RS 4
.nf
.fam C
octa \-\-hash\-columns customer_id,order_date orders.parquet \-f csv > keyed.csv
octa \-\-hash\-columns first,last,birth \-\-hash\-algo sha256 \-\-hash\-trim \-\-hash\-upper people.csv
.fam
.fi
.if n .RE
.RE
.SS "Combine and split"
.sp
One workbook from several tables
.RS 4
.sp
.if n .RS 4
.nf
.fam C
octa \-\-to\-workbook report.xlsx sales.csv returns.parquet stock.json
.fam
.fi
.if n .RE
.RE
.sp
Fold a folder of parts into one file
.RS 4
.sp
.if n .RS 4
.nf
.fam C
octa \-\-harmonise\-schema ./parts \-\-combine \-\-out ./all\-parts.csv
.fam
.fi
.if n .RE
.RE
.SS "Convert and SQL"
.sp
Convert formats
.RS 4
.sp
.if n .RS 4
.nf
.fam C
octa \-\-convert in.csv out.parquet
octa \-\-convert workbook.xlsx tidy.sqlite
.fam
.fi
.if n .RE
.RE
.sp
Group\-by aggregation
.RS 4
.sp
.if n .RS 4
.nf
.fam C
octa \-\-sql sales.parquet \-q \*(AqSELECT region, SUM(amount) FROM data GROUP BY region\*(Aq
.fam
.fi
.if n .RE
.RE
.sp
Read every row of a huge file
.RS 4
.sp
.if n .RS 4
.nf
.fam C
octa \-\-sql huge.parquet \-q \*(AqSELECT count(*) FROM data\*(Aq \-\-rows all
octa \-\-head huge.parquet \-n 100 \-\-rows 10,000,000
.fam
.fi
.if n .RE
.RE
.sp
Pipe a SQL result through \fBjq\fP
.RS 4
.sp
.if n .RS 4
.nf
.fam C
octa \-\-sql users.parquet \-q \*(AqSELECT email FROM data WHERE active\*(Aq \-f json | jq \-r \*(Aq.[].email\*(Aq
.fam
.fi
.if n .RE
.RE
.sp
JOIN across formats
.RS 4
.sp
.if n .RS 4
.nf
.fam C
octa \-\-sql sales.parquet \(rs
     \-\-sql\-table customers=customers.csv \(rs
     \-q \*(AqSELECT c.name, SUM(d.amount) FROM data d
         JOIN customers c ON d.cid = c.cid GROUP BY c.name\*(Aq
.fam
.fi
.if n .RE
.RE
.sp
ATTACH a DuckDB warehouse and JOIN against it
.RS 4
.sp
.if n .RS 4
.nf
.fam C
octa \-\-sql sales.parquet \(rs
     \-\-sql\-attach wh=warehouse.duckdb \(rs
     \-q \*(AqSELECT count(*) FROM data d
         JOIN wh.main.products p ON d.cid = p.cid\*(Aq
.fam
.fi
.if n .RE
.RE
.sp
Write a SQL result back to a DuckDB schema
.RS 4
.sp
.if n .RS 4
.nf
.fam C
octa \-\-sql sales.parquet \-q \*(AqSELECT region, SUM(amount) AS total
                             FROM data GROUP BY region\*(Aq \(rs
     \-\-sql\-write\-to analytics.duckdb \(rs
     \-\-sql\-write\-schema reports \-\-sql\-write\-table q4_summary
.fam
.fi
.if n .RE
.RE
.sp
Export a schema as Snowflake DDL
.RS 4
.sp
.if n .RS 4
.nf
.fam C
octa \-\-export\-schema sales.parquet \-t snowflake
octa \-e users.parquet \-t pydantic > users_model.py
.fam
.fi
.if n .RE
.RE
.SS "Databases"
.sp
Browse a live database connection
.RS 4
.sp
.if n .RS 4
.nf
.fam C
octa \-\-db\-tables \-\-db warehouse
octa \-\-db\-tables \-\-db warehouse \-\-db\-catalog sales_prod
octa \-\-db\-query "SELECT * FROM public.users LIMIT 10" \-\-db prod
.fam
.fi
.if n .RE
.RE
.sp
Review a change as SQL before it touches the server
.RS 4
.sp
.if n .RS 4
.nf
.fam C
octa \-\-sync\-sql users.csv \-\-db prod \-\-sync\-table public.users \-\-sync\-on id > change.sql
.fam
.fi
.if n .RE
.RE
.sp
Copy a table between two servers, replacing the target
.RS 4
.sp
.if n .RS 4
.nf
.fam C
octa \-\-db prod \-\-db\-copy analytics.orders \(rs
     \-\-db\-copy\-to warehouse \-\-db\-write\-mode replace
.fam
.fi
.if n .RE
.RE
.SS "Cloud and web"
.sp
.if n .RS 4
.nf
.fam C
octa \-\-cloud\-ls s3://bucket/prefix/
octa \-\-cloud\-ls s3://bucket/ \-\-recursive

octa \-\-cloud\-get s3://bucket/data.parquet \-\-out ./data.parquet
octa \-\-cloud\-put ./data.parquet \-\-to s3://bucket/data.parquet

octa \-\-cloud\-copy s3://a/data/ \-\-to gs://b/backup/
octa \-\-cloud\-move s3://a/old.csv \-\-to s3://a/archive/old.csv
octa \-\-cloud\-delete s3://a/old.csv
octa \-\-cloud\-delete s3://a/scratch/ \-\-recursive

# A saved REST/JSON endpoint, every page of it
octa \-\-api Orders
octa \-\-api Orders \-\-api\-path /customers \-f csv > customers.csv
.fam
.fi
.if n .RE
.SS "Connections"
.sp
.if n .RS 4
.nf
.fam C
octa \-\-list\-connections

export S3_KEY=\*(AqAKIAEXAMPLE:wJalrXUtnFEMI\*(Aq
octa \-\-add\-connection \*(Aqkind=s3,name=prod,bucket=my\-bucket,region=eu\-central\-1,allow_writes=true\*(Aq \(rs
     \-\-secret\-env S3_KEY

octa \-\-add\-connection \*(Aqkind=postgres,name=warehouse,host=db.internal,database=analytics,user=reader\*(Aq \(rs
     \-\-secret\-env PGPASSWORD

octa \-\-remove\-connection warehouse
.fam
.fi
.if n .RE
.SS "MCP server"
.sp
Start the MCP server
.RS 4
.sp
.if n .RS 4
.nf
.fam C
octa \-\-mcp
octa \-\-mcp \-\-mcp\-read\-only \-\-mcp\-tools core,quality
.fam
.fi
.if n .RE
.RE
.SS "Pipelines"
.sp
\f(CR\-\fP stands for standard input as a \fIFILE\fP, and for standard output as the
\f(CR\-\-convert\fP target, so octa fits in the middle of a shell pipeline.
.sp
.if n .RS 4
.nf
.fam C
# Read a table from a pipe: the format is sniffed from the bytes
curl \-s https://example.org/sales.csv | octa \-\-schema \-

# Query it without ever writing a file
cat sales.csv | octa \-\-sql \- \-q "SELECT city, SUM(amount) FROM data GROUP BY city"

# Convert on the way through; \-\-to says what to write, since a pipe has no name
cat sales.csv | octa \-\-convert \- \- \-\-to json | jq \*(Aq.[0]\*(Aq
.fam
.fi
.if n .RE
.sp
Reports and counts go to stderr, so what reaches the next command in the
pipeline is exactly the data. Piped input is buffered to a temporary file
first: every reader needs to seek (a Parquet footer is at the end of the
file), so a pipe cannot be streamed straight through.
.SS "Reading from a URL"
.sp
Anywhere a \fIFILE\fP is accepted, a URL works too.
.sp
.if n .RS 4
.nf
.fam C
# A plain web address: downloaded to a temp file, no configuration needed
octa \-\-schema https://example.org/exports/sales.csv
octa \-\-sql https://example.org/exports/sales.csv \-q "SELECT * FROM data LIMIT 5"

# Cloud object storage resolves through the saved connections instead
octa \-\-schema s3://bucket/sales.parquet
.fam
.fi
.if n .RE
.sp
The format comes from the URL\(cqs path, ignoring any query string, so a signed
link ending \f(CRsales.csv?token=.\|.\|.\fP still reads as CSV; a URL with no extension
falls back to content sniffing. Both forms are read\-only, and a non\-2xx
response is an error naming the status code rather than a file, so a 404 HTML
page is never parsed as a one\-column table.
.sp
Tool\-supplied URLs are restricted. When the address comes from an agent (the
MCP server or the in\-app assistant) rather than from you, Octa resolves the
host first and refuses anything that is not globally routable: loopback,
private ranges, link\-local (which includes the cloud metadata endpoint at
169.254.169.254), and unique\-local IPv6. Redirects are not followed on that
path either, since a redirect is how a check on the first hop gets bypassed.
Addresses you type yourself, on the command line or in File > Open URL, are
not restricted: reading from \f(CRlocalhost:8000\fP is a normal thing to ask for.
.SH "ENVIRONMENT"
.sp
\fBOCTA_CONFIG_DIR\fP
.RS 4
Directory holding \fBsettings.toml\fP, used verbatim on every platform and
taking precedence over \fBXDG_CONFIG_HOME\fP / \fBHOME\fP / \fBAPPDATA\fP. Required in
containers, which usually set none of those; without it Octa reports that
it has no config directory rather than silently running without settings.
.RE
.sp
\fBOCTA_DEBUG\fP
.RS 4
Set to 1 to force debug mode on for one run without changing the saved
setting: verbose logging, plus one log line per mouse press and release
recording its position, whether it counted as a click, and which layer of
the interface it reached. Intended for diagnosing the GUI itself, when the
Settings dialog is the thing misbehaving and its checkbox cannot be reached.
A build from source additionally outlines every clickable area on screen;
release binaries cannot, since the toolkit compiles that drawing out.
.RE
.sp
\fBOCTA_NO_KEYRING\fP
.RS 4
Set to 1 to skip the OS keyring entirely, so secrets are read from and
written to \fBsettings.toml\fP (chmod 0600). Secrets already fall back to the
file when the keyring errors; this avoids the lookup on systems where it
can never work, such as a container with no D\-Bus.
.RE
.sp
\fBAWS_ACCESS_KEY_ID\fP, \fBAWS_SECRET_ACCESS_KEY\fP, \fBAZURE_STORAGE_ACCOUNT\fP, \fBGOOGLE_APPLICATION_CREDENTIALS\fP
.RS 4
Ambient cloud credentials, used when no saved connection covers a URL.
.RE
.SH "FILES"
.sp
\f(CR\fI$XDG_CONFIG_HOME/octa/settings.toml\fP\fP
.RS 4
Linux. User settings. Created on first launch with defaults.
.RE
.sp
\f(CR\fI$HOME/Library/Application Support/Octa/settings.toml\fP\fP
.RS 4
macOS. Same purpose.
.RE
.sp
\f(CR\fI%APPDATA%\(rsOcta\(rssettings.toml\fP\fP
.RS 4
Windows. Same purpose.
.RE
